{
  "for_the_owner": "The proposed UR5e cobot will handle sliding the power bank across the table into the target staging zone using a standard parallel gripper. The operator retains the bimanual lifting and visual quality inspection of the device casing. At an estimated net investment of 90,000 SGD, the expected payback period is 58.4 months based on modest cycle time reductions from 5.0 to 4.15 seconds. The primary caveat is that automatable scope is currently minimal, meaning economic justification is weak unless more tasks like fixturing and inspection are brought into the cell.",
  "step_notes": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "recommendation": "Install a mechanical nesting bracket or gravity-fed locating nest on the table so the incoming power bank arrives in a deterministic orientation for picking or sliding.",
      "fixture": "Mechanical corner-stop nest or passive locating tray with guide bevels"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "recommendation": "Automate with a UR5e equipped with a standard two-finger parallel gripper (Robotiq 2F-85 class) using compliant linear jogging along the tabletop to shift the 280 g power bank to the target mark.",
      "fixture": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "recommendation": "Keep with the operator because mid-air bimanual reorientation and visual inspection cannot be replicated by a single parallel gripper without adding an expensive multi-camera vision system.",
      "fixture": ""
    }
  ],
  "risks": [
    {
      "title": "Poor economic return",
      "detail": "Automating only a 1.65-second sliding step yields minimal labor savings, leading to a long 58.4-month payback. Verify whether adjacent feeding or packing steps can be bundled before issuing purchase orders."
    },
    {
      "title": "Part surface scratching",
      "detail": "Sliding the bare power bank across table surfaces and guide stops risks cosmetic scuffs on the housing. Test tabletop materials using Delrin or UHMW-PE sheets and soft silicone gripper pads before final cell build."
    },
    {
      "title": "Loose part misalignment",
      "detail": "Without active sensing or fixed stops, slight variance in drop placement will cause gripper collisions or miss-picks. Validate that a passive mechanical nest repeatedly seats the 140x70x20 mm casing within 1 mm."
    }
  ],
  "next_steps": [
    "Fabricate a rapid 3D-printed locating nest for the 140x70x20 mm power bank and verify manual loading repeatability.",
    "Benchmark slide friction and surface wear on prototype power bank casings using polyurethane or silicone gripper fingers.",
    "Obtain turnkey quotation packages for a UR5e arm, Robotiq 2F-85 gripper, and basic base framing.",
    "Re-evaluate station workflow to determine if pre-kitting from bins A, B, and C can be integrated to improve project payback."
  ],
  "questions": [
    "Is a 58.4-month payback acceptable to management for automating only the sliding task?",
    "Can the upstream placement of the power bank be mechanically fixed so step s1 can also be automated?",
